A||||15||Galen||Religion? Faith? Belief? My IDEAS on the subject..||||4||
Z||000000||Galen||02-27-2001||09:41 PM||||<I>my disclaimer... before anyone emails me with flames and stuff, and you know you want to, dont. Thank you.</I><P><B>Most</B>, if not all religions, including every differant kind of christian there is, say that you must have faith in your belief. I know comedy movies aren't meant to make sense, but the movie Dogma brought up a good idea. A belief is hard to change, but an idea isn't. You should have an idea about faith, not a belief. &lt;-- ranting<P>Not directed at anyone, but you'll know if you said it. Mutations arn't constructive? just distructive?  okie I admit, a mutated virus, distructive. But scientists have made 'mutations' of antivirus's to fight viruses that afflict humans. I mean, is that not constructive? Would you be ashamed to say that you were mutated from a Ape? or a Monkey perhaps? arn't they some of the smartest 'animals'?. arn't humans animals still? or have we mutated beyond that?<P>Theres too much contridiction in all the differant religions for ANY of them to be true. One says the other is wrong, and then another says the one that just said it was wrong is wrong. Who's to say who's right? No one can. Therefore I'm not saying any are correct. But you can't say any aren't.||||reg||4
Z||000001||Standard||02-28-2001||12:50 AM||||Actually, some of the most intelligent organism's on the earth are most likely the dolphins  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  not kidding, either.. but all I have to say is...<P>"<I>So long, and thanks for all the fish</I>."<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cheever2||03-01-2001||12:02 AM||||Galen, you pose some deep things there, and first of all, I respect your right to your opinion and your freedom to say it.  Please respect my right to disagree, and my freedom to voice that as well.  Why can't a person stand for something whole-heartedly without someone getting offended by that stance?  But if each will respect the other's rights, we should all get along. <P>You mentioned having a "faith in your belief", and a belief being hard to change.  Why should every belief you have be subject to change?  I don't know how anyone could live like that.  I'm glad that I have some beliefs that are unchangeable.  As far as humans being animals, that seems to be the consensus on this Forum, but I'll just say I do not believe humans to be animals, period.  Not now, not ever, having no relation whatsoever.  Definitely not scientific, apparently, but my firm belief nonetheless.  I don't know how anyone could live with the notion that the earthly grave is the end.  Thirdly, your statements regarding what's true and what's wrong...  Here's my response, and again I don't expect many to agree, but I am compelled to speak my mind (I don't often get serious here, so here's a rare moment):<P>First, if you do not believe this existence is from a creator with a purpose, then this debate must be a waste of time to you, except for causing you to respond to what you surely perceive as arrogance by those who do believe.  If you are solid in that unbelief, fair enough, the rest of this is not for you.  <P>By definition, <B>there can only be one truth</B>.<P>Now, why would the Creator keep truth & purpose a complete mystery to everybody?  I don't buy that.  SOMEBODY has an unshakeable grasp on what it's all about, and some are still searching.  If you're still searching, my advise is don't stop, but do your searching with a strategy that is more than just floating around on the wind.  Those who are absolutely certain that they have found the truth will want to share it (and should), yet will naturally be the object of attacks from unbelievers, first for the arrogance to say they found it, second for trying to share it.  Some of those believers are more "pushy" than others, as will be found in any group adhering to any purpose, but if their persuasion is not done with compassion then it will be contrary to the Creator's nature.  But if there are indeed moral principles which are not to be broken without consequence, then those believers' steadfast unbending will be within the Creator's desires, regardless of anyone's contempt.  I won't come on here and say any specific group (a.k.a. any "organized religion" or "denomination") has the truth, and all others do not, because such a blanket statement cannot be entirely true.  But I have found some with whom I agree on fundamental issues of truth and of life's purpose.  I personally am not a good persuader of others.  And even if I were, I would respect their freedoms as much as I would want them to respect mine.  But mostly my point in this is that you are incorrect to say that everybody is wrong, that there is a mixture of right and wrong in everyone.  There IS a truth to be heard and heeded, and anything which has any contradiction whatsoever with the truth is to be dismissed.  And there are people who profess to have found that truth, although being human we are not able to live up to it 24/7.  And that helps to demonstrate the perfection of the Creator.<P>Well, this may not be profound to anyone, but there it is.  I will add that I do believe the Bible, and I am one of those who believe it literally.  Disagree if you will, as the vast majority of 6 billion people on this planet do.<P>I only made one post on the evolution topic, and I'll only make this one here.  There's more to say, but I don't like to read long posts (even if I agree with them) so I don't like to write them long either.  This is all controversial stuff, but I'll have a hot dog with any of you anytime, okay?||||reg||6
Z||000003||Phaeton||03-01-2001||02:39 AM||||Truth is very hard to find.<BR>Discussing the search while enjoying a hot dog is somewhat more readily available, but not everywhere.<BR>And if I parked my Fiero next to another Fiero while visiting, well, not much could top all that.<BR>Thank you for the offer.||||reg||12
